  "ssriPageTitle": "SSRI Recovery",
  "ssriHeaderStarted": "Your Recovery Journey",
  "ssriHeaderNotStarted": "Benefits of Tapering Off SSRIs",
  "ssriSubtitleStarted": "Track your progress and celebrate each milestone",
  "ssriSubtitleNotStarted": "See what happens when you taper off",
  "ssriMilestone3Title": "Day 3: FINISH Syndrome Peaks",
  "ssriMilestone3Description": "Withdrawal symptoms often peak around days 2–4. SSRIs change how serotonin is recycled, so the brain needs time to adjust after you stop. Expect flu-like symptoms, dizziness, insomnia, nausea, and 'brain zaps'. Paroxetine tends to cause stronger withdrawal; fluoxetine lasts much longer in the body and is usually milder.",
  "ssriMilestone7Title": "One Week: Acute Phase Beginning to Ease",
  "ssriMilestone7Description": "The most intense physical symptoms are beginning to subside. Your brain is adjusting its serotonin signalling to function without the medication. Sleep and appetite are beginning to stabilise.",
  "ssriMilestone14Title": "Two Weeks: Physical Symptoms Resolving",
  "ssriMilestone14Description": "Most acute physical discontinuation symptoms have significantly reduced or resolved. Brain zaps, nausea, and dizziness are fading. Emotional sensitivity may still be heightened as your brain continues to rebalance its serotonin systems.",
  "ssriMilestone30Title": "One Month: Mood and Energy Stabilising",
  "ssriMilestone30Description": "One month marks a significant milestone. Sleep quality is improving, energy levels are stabilising, and many people notice their mood beginning to find a new equilibrium. The brain is actively restoring its natural serotonin regulation.",
  "ssriMilestone90Title": "Three Months: Serotonin Receptor Recovery",
  "ssriMilestone90Description": "Three months is a major recovery milestone. The serotonin system has had months to adjust after the medicine is gone. Mood, anxiety, focus, and clear thinking are meaningfully better for most people.",
  "ssriMilestone180Title": "Six Months: Long-Term Brain Adjustment",
  "ssriMilestone180Description": "Six months allows for substantial neurological adjustment. The brain's serotonin systems have found their natural balance. Many people report improved emotional range and, for those who experienced it, sexual dysfunction caused by SSRIs has typically resolved.",
  "ssriMilestone365Title": "One Year: Recovery Achieved",
  "ssriMilestone365Description": "One year marks a complete cycle of recovery. Research shows the majority who completed a gradual taper and received psychological support remain well at one year, with quality of life comparable or better than those who remained on medication.",
  "settingsShowSsriTracking": "Show SSRI tracking",
  "settingsNotifySsri": "Notify SSRI quitting progress",
  "addictionSnri": "SNRIs",
  "@addictionSnri": {
    "description": "SNRI addiction name"
  },
  "snriPageTitle": "SNRI Recovery",
  "snriHeaderStarted": "Your Recovery Journey",
  "snriHeaderNotStarted": "Benefits of Tapering Off SNRIs",
  "snriSubtitleStarted": "Track your progress and celebrate each milestone",
  "snriSubtitleNotStarted": "See what happens when you taper off",
  "snriMilestone3Title": "Day 3: Intense Discontinuation Syndrome Peaks",
  "snriMilestone3Description": "SNRIs — particularly venlafaxine (Effexor) — are associated with some of the most severe antidepressant discontinuation syndromes. Venlafaxine's very short half-life (5 hours) means symptoms can be more intense than with most SSRIs. FINISH symptoms (flu-like feelings, insomnia, nausea, imbalance, sensory disturbances, hyperarousal) affect both serotonin and norepinephrine systems simultaneously.",
  "snriMilestone7Title": "One Week: Two Brain-Chemical Systems Adjusting",
  "snriMilestone7Description": "SNRIs affect both serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake. Both systems are adjusting simultaneously. The most intense physical symptoms are beginning to subside, but the dual mechanism means adjustment can feel more complex than with SSRIs alone.",
  "snriMilestone14Title": "Two Weeks: Physical Symptoms Fading",
  "snriMilestone14Description": "Most acute physical discontinuation symptoms have significantly reduced. Brain zaps, nausea, and dizziness are fading. The norepinephrine system's readjustment may still be causing some anxiety or blood pressure fluctuations — these typically resolve over coming weeks.",
  "snriMilestone30Title": "One Month: Norepinephrine System Stabilising",
  "snriMilestone30Description": "One month marks a significant milestone. Both serotonin and norepinephrine systems are actively rebalancing. Sleep quality and energy levels are stabilising. Many people notice improved emotional range as the medication's effect on emotional blunting begins to resolve.",
  "snriMilestone90Title": "Three Months: Dual System Rebalancing",
  "snriMilestone90Description": "Three months is a major recovery milestone. The serotonin and noradrenaline systems have had months to adjust. Mood, anxiety, focus, and clear thinking are meaningfully better after a supervised taper.",
  "snriMilestone180Title": "Six Months: Neurological Recovery",
  "snriMilestone180Description": "Six months allows for substantial readjustment of both serotonin and norepinephrine systems. The HPA axis — which SNRIs influence through norepinephrine — is normalising. Many people report notably improved resilience to everyday stressors at this point.",
  "snriMilestone365Title": "One Year: Recovery Achieved",
  "snriMilestone365Description": "One year marks a complete cycle of dual serotonin-norepinephrine recovery. Research consistently shows that people who successfully discontinue SNRIs under medical supervision and with psychological support maintain good outcomes at one year.",
  "settingsShowSnriTracking": "Show SNRI tracking",
  "settingsNotifySnri": "Notify SNRI quitting progress",
  "addictionTca": "TCAs",
  "@addictionTca": {
    "description": "Tricyclic antidepressant addiction name"
  },
  "tcaPageTitle": "TCA Recovery",
  "tcaHeaderStarted": "Your Recovery Journey",
  "tcaHeaderNotStarted": "Benefits of Tapering Off TCAs",
  "tcaSubtitleStarted": "Track your progress and celebrate each milestone",
  "tcaSubtitleNotStarted": "See what happens when you taper off",
  "tcaMilestone3Title": "Day 3: Acetylcholine Rebound Peaks",
  "tcaMilestone3Description": "TCAs block acetylcholine. After stopping, that system can briefly become overactive. Symptoms include nausea, diarrhoea, stomach cramps, heavy sweating, headache, muscle aches, and insomnia. These symptoms usually peak early and then ease.",
  "tcaMilestone7Title": "One Week: Rebound Symptoms Easing",
  "tcaMilestone7Description": "The acetylcholine rebound usually peaks in the first few days and starts easing by one week. Stomach symptoms, sweating, and flu-like feelings are reducing, while the body systems that control heart rate, digestion, and sweating return toward normal.",
  "tcaMilestone14Title": "Two Weeks: Anticholinergic Effects Reversing",
  "tcaMilestone14Description": "The dry mouth, constipation, trouble urinating, and blurred vision caused by TCAs are reversing as the acetylcholine system recovers. Memory and attention often feel clearer too.",
  "tcaMilestone30Title": "One Month: Autonomic Nervous System Stabilising",
  "tcaMilestone30Description": "At one month, the body systems that control heart rate, blood pressure, digestion, and sweating are settling. Heart rhythm and blood-pressure changes are moving toward normal, and REM dream sleep is returning.",
  "tcaMilestone90Title": "Three Months: Brain-Chemical Systems Settling",
  "tcaMilestone90Description": "TCAs affect several brain-chemical systems. Three months gives them substantial time to settle. Mood, clear thinking, and physical wellbeing are markedly better.",
  "tcaMilestone180Title": "Six Months: Heart and Thinking Recovery",
  "tcaMilestone180Description": "Six months allows major recovery in heart rhythm, memory, and thinking speed. The heart-rhythm changes and brain fog caused by TCAs have had months to resolve.",
  "tcaMilestone365Title": "One Year: Recovery Achieved",
  "tcaMilestone365Description": "One year gives every system affected by TCAs a full year to recover. The acetylcholine, noradrenaline, serotonin, and histamine systems have settled, and memory, clear thinking, and heart-rate control are substantially recovered.",
  "settingsShowTcaTracking": "Show TCA tracking",
  "settingsNotifyTca": "Notify TCA quitting progress",
  "addictionMaoi": "MAOIs",
  "@addictionMaoi": {
    "description": "MAOI addiction name"
  },
  "maoiPageTitle": "MAOI Recovery",
  "maoiHeaderStarted": "Your Recovery Journey",
  "maoiHeaderNotStarted": "Benefits of Tapering Off MAOIs",
  "maoiSubtitleStarted": "Track your progress and celebrate each milestone",
  "maoiSubtitleNotStarted": "See what happens when you taper off",
  "maoiMilestone3Title": "Day 3: Highest Risk Phase — Medical Supervision Essential",
  "maoiMilestone3Description": "MAOIs carry the most serious discontinuation risks of any antidepressant class. Abrupt cessation — especially of phenelzine and tranylcypromine — can cause severe agitation, confusion, hallucinations, myoclonus, and hyperthermia. If you are experiencing these symptoms, seek immediate medical attention. MAOIs work by permanently disabling MAO enzymes; recovery requires the body to synthesise new enzyme, a process taking approximately 14 days.",
  "maoiMilestone7Title": "One Week: MAO Enzyme Synthesis Underway",
  "maoiMilestone7Description": "MAOIs permanently destroy MAO enzyme molecules; recovery requires synthesising entirely new enzyme. By one week, approximately 50% of normal MAO activity may have recovered. Severe acute discontinuation symptoms (delirium, myoclonus, hyperthermia) are substantially reduced. Close medical supervision remains important.",
  "maoiMilestone14Title": "Two Weeks: MAO Enzyme Activity Substantially Recovered",
  "maoiMilestone14Description": "Two weeks is the standard clinical timeframe for MAO enzyme recovery after discontinuation. By approximately day 14, MAO-A and MAO-B activity is largely restored and normal tyramine metabolism is re-established. The dietary restrictions required during MAOI treatment can typically be relaxed in consultation with your prescriber.",
  "maoiMilestone30Title": "One Month: Brain-Chemical Systems Rebalancing",
  "maoiMilestone30Description": "One month after stopping an MAOI, the MAO enzyme is active again and the brain is handling serotonin, noradrenaline, dopamine, and tyramine naturally. The special food and drug interaction period has ended, though you should still tell prescribers about previous MAOI use.",
  "maoiMilestone90Title": "Three Months: Brain Chemistry Normalising",
  "maoiMilestone90Description": "Three months gives the brain's mood and energy systems substantial time to readjust after MAOIs. Mood, energy, and clear thinking are stabilising as the brain handles these chemicals naturally again.",
  "maoiMilestone180Title": "Six Months: Long-Term Recovery",
  "maoiMilestone180Description": "Six months represents a substantial period of natural monoamine function. Serotonin, norepinephrine, and dopamine receptor populations have normalised. The phenethylamine (PEA) pathways also affected by MAOIs have restored to natural levels — an often-overlooked aspect of recovery that contributes to emotional normalisation.",
  "maoiMilestone365Title": "One Year: Recovery Achieved",
  "maoiMilestone365Description": "One year after stopping an MAOI gives the brain and body a full year without the drug. Mood, energy, clear thinking, and normal brain-chemical regulation have had substantial time to settle and recover.",
  "settingsShowMaoiTracking": "Show MAOI tracking",
  "settingsNotifyMaoi": "Notify MAOI quitting progress",
